\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Number Systems and Codes}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item write numbers in binary, octal and hexadecimal;
  \item convert between decimal, binary, octal and hexadecimal;
  \item explain the purpose of binary coded decimal (BCD);
  \item perform simple BCD encoding and decoding;
  \item understand the basic idea of Gray code and how to convert to and from binary.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Binary system}

The \term{binary} system uses only the two digits 0 and 1. Each position represents a power of two, starting with \$2^{0}$ at the right most digit. To find the decimal value, multiply each digit by its power of two and add the results.

\begin{example}
Convert \$11010_{2}$ to decimal.
\[
1\cdot2^{4}+1\cdot2^{3}+0\cdot2^{2}+1\cdot2^{1}+0\cdot2^{0}=16+8+0+2+0=26_{10}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Octal system}

The \term{octal} system uses the digits 0 through 7. Each position represents a power of eight. Conversion to decimal follows the same pattern as binary, but with powers of eight.

For example, \$157_{8}$ equals:
\[
1\cdot8^{2}+5\cdot8^{1}+7\cdot8^{0}=64+40+7=111_{10}.
\]

\subsection{Hexadecimal system}

The \term{hexadecimal} system uses sixteen symbols: the digits 0--9 and the letters A--F, where A represents ten, B eleven, up to F fifteen. Each position represents a power of sixteen.

For example, \$2B_{16}$ equals:
\[
2\cdot16^{1}+11\cdot16^{0}=32+11=43_{10}.
\]

\subsection{Conversions between bases}

\subsubsection{Decimal to binary}

Repeatedly divide the decimal number by two, recording the remainder each time. The binary representation is the list of remainders read from bottom to top.

\subsubsection{Binary to octal}

Group binary digits in sets of three, starting from the right. Replace each group with its octal equivalent.

\subsubsection{Binary to hexadecimal}

Group binary digits in sets of four, starting from the right. Replace each group with its hexadecimal equivalent.

\begin{example}
Convert \$10111011_{2}$ to hexadecimal.
\[
1011\ 1011\rightarrow B\ B_{16}=BB_{16}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Octal or hexadecimal to binary}

Replace each octal digit by its three bit binary equivalent, or each hexadecimal digit by its four bit binary equivalent.

\subsection{Binary coded decimal (BCD)}

\term{Binary coded decimal} stores each decimal digit separately as a four bit binary number. For example, the decimal number 47 is stored as \$0100\ 0111$ (4 = 0100, 7 = 0111). This representation is useful in devices that need to display decimal digits directly, such as digital clocks and calculators.

\begin{example}
Encode the decimal number 259 in BCD.
\[
2\rightarrow0010,\;5\rightarrow0101,\;9\rightarrow1001,\; \text{so }259_{10}=0010\ 0101\ 1001_{BCD}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Gray code}

The \term{Gray code} is a binary numeral system where two successive values differ in only one bit. This property reduces errors in digital communication and position encoding. To convert a binary number to Gray code, keep the most significant bit unchanged and XOR each subsequent bit with the bit to its left. Converting back to binary requires a cumulative XOR from the most significant bit downwards.

\begin{example}
Convert binary \$1010_{2}$ to Gray code.
\[
\text{Most significant bit stays }1.\\
\text{Next bit }0\oplus1=1.\\
\text{Next bit }1\oplus0=1.\\
\text{Last bit }0\oplus1=1.\\
\text{Resulting Gray code }1111_{G}.
\]
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Convert \$101101_{2}$ to octal and hexadecimal, then encode the decimal result in BCD.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write the decimal number 45 in binary, octal and hexadecimal.
  \item Convert \$7F_{16}$ to decimal and then to binary.
  \item Encode the decimal number 83 in BCD and decode the BCD \$1000\ 0011$ back to decimal.
  \item Convert the binary number \$101101_{2}$ to octal, to hexadecimal, and then encode the decimal result in BCD.
  \item Convert binary \$1010_{2}$ to Gray code and back to binary.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Binary & Base 2 number system using digits 0 and 1. \\
Octal & Base 8 number system using digits 0--7. \\
Hexadecimal & Base 16 number system using digits 0--9 and A--F. \\
Conversion & Process of changing a number from one base to another. \\
Binary coded decimal (BCD) & Representation of each decimal digit by a four bit binary code. \\
Gray code & Binary code where successive values differ in only one bit. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
